          "case_name": "DeShaney v. Winnebago County Department of Social Services",
          "citation": "489 U.S. 189 (1989)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1989,
          "date_decided": "1989-02-22",
          "holding": "The Supreme Court held that the Due Process Clause imposed no constitutional duty on county social services officials to protect a child from his father's violence where the child remained in his father's custody rather than state custody.",
          "relevance": "It is the reason child fatality accountability in the United States proceeds through review commissions and state tort law rather than federal constitutional suits.",
          "quote": "the Due Process Clauses generally confer no affirmative right to governmental aid, even where such aid may be necessary to secure life, liberty, or property interests",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112202/deshaney-v-winnebago-cty-dept-of-social-servs/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112202
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Mercer v. South Dakota Attorney General Office",
          "citation": "2015 S.D. 31, 864 N.W.2d 299",
          "court": "South Dakota Supreme Court",
          "year": 2015,
          "date_decided": "2015-05-13",
          "holding": "The court affirmed denial of a public records request for a death investigation file, holding SDCL 1-27-1.5(5) and SDCL 23-5-11 expressly exempt law enforcement investigative reports and confidential criminal justice information, and that no right to redaction or partial release exists.",
          "relevance": "A death investigation is precisely the record category held exempt here, which is the central obstacle to independent child fatality review.",
          "quote": "The records developed or received by the agencies charged with the duty to investigate Benda's death constitute confidential criminal justice information as defined by statute.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2800709/mercer-v-south-dakota-attorney-general-office/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2800709
        },
        {
          "case_name": "E.P. v. Riley",
          "citation": "1999 S.D. 163, 604 N.W.2d 7",
          "court": "South Dakota Supreme Court",
          "year": 1999,
          "date_decided": "1999-12-29",
          "holding": "The court held that DSS employees owed a common law duty to protect a child from a foster child with known dangerous propensities and reversed summary judgment because fact issues remained on whether the employees acted in good faith under SDCL 26-8A-14.",
          "relevance": "The court's reliance on DSS's own procedures manual and administrative rules shows how internal standards become evidence in a post-harm review.",
          "quote": "it is clear the immunity provisions of SDCL 26-8A-14 apply to the placement activities of DSS employees involving JH",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/900372/ep-v-riley/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 900372
        }
